# Files Fern must not modify or delete when regenerating this repo.
#
# Fern owns the SDK source and the ci.yml beside this workflow, and prunes anything it
# did not generate — it reads this file from the pull request's base branch, so an entry
# only protects a file once it is on main.
#
# publish-main.yml is hand-written. Go has no registry: distribution is this repository,
# served to consumers through proxy.golang.org, so "publishing" is pushing a vX.Y.Z tag.
# The workflow releases whopsdk-go when a merge to main lands a core/request_option.go
# whose X-Fern-SDK-Version stamp is not yet tagged. It needs no credential beyond the
# GITHUB_TOKEN that pushes the tag.
#
# Two consequences of that shape are irreversible and worth stating here:
#
#   * proxy.golang.org caches a version permanently and immutably. Once it has served
#     github.com/whopio/whopsdk-go@vX.Y.Z, moving the tag changes nothing for anyone who
#     already fetched it, and the mistake is invisible locally. The workflow refuses to
#     move an existing tag and refuses to release a version the proxy already serves.
#
#   * The module path in go.mod is the import path. It must equal the repository URL, and
#     from v2 onwards it must carry a /vN suffix or every consumer breaks. The workflow
#     checks both against the version it is about to tag.
#
# The Fern Go generator's ci.yml only compiles, lints and tests. It contains no
# tag-triggered publish job and no id-token: write — unlike the Ruby generator's, per
# whopio/whopsdk-ruby#55 — so it is left to Fern rather than being taken over here.
#
# LICENSE is listed pre-emptively: Fern does not carry one over, and a public Go module
# without one is not safely consumable.
#
# webhook_verification.go is the hand-written UnwrapWebhook helper, and
# webhook_verification_test.go is its test — Fern owns *_test.go too, so without the second
# entry a regeneration would prune the test and leave the helper untested rather than fail
# loudly. It restores the verification half of `client.webhooks.unwrap`, which the
# Stainless-generated SDK shipped and Fern cannot generate: Fern generates from OpenAPI
# paths and `unwrap` was never a path.
#
# It sits in the root package rather than a package of its own so the import path a
# consumer uses is the module path itself — the one path here that no regeneration can
# move. It is a package-level function, not a method on a generated client, so nothing
# generated is patched.
#
# Unlike whopsdk-ruby, whopsdk-python and whopsdk-typescript, this needs nothing restored
# from whop-monorepo's sdks/fern/generators.yml. Those three each keep a helper alive with
# two entries there — a require/export path, because their entry point is generated, and an
# extraDependencies entry, because their manifest is generated and the helper needs
# standardwebhooks. Go needs neither: a file in a package is compiled by virtue of being in
# the directory, so there is nothing to register, and the helper is standard library only
# (crypto/hmac, crypto/sha256), so the generated go.mod — regenerated on 8af0c93 — needs no
# declaration it would lose.
.github/workflows/publish-main.yml
LICENSE
webhook_verification.go
webhook_verification_test.go
